];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens communicate with each other?', a: 'Yes — ravens communicate using a large repertoire of vocalisations (40–80+ distinct call types in Common Ravens) as well as physical gestures (bill-pointing, wing displays). Their calls include alarm calls distinguishing aerial from ground threats, food-recruitment calls, territorial vocalisations, pair-bond maintenance calls, and vocalisations in social conflict. Some calls appear referential — they carry specific information about the type of threat or food present. Ravens also communicate using gaze and gesturing, rare in non-human animals outside of primates.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens have referential calls?', a: 'Ravens show evidence of referential calls — vocalisations that refer to specific objects or events rather than merely expressing emotional state. Alarm calls differ based on predator type (aerial vs. ground), and naive ravens respond appropriately to recorded alarm calls without having seen the predator. Food calls appear to recruit conspecifics to food sources, with some evidence of specificity. However, raven communication appears to lack two key properties of human language: unlimited productivity (the ability to combine elements to create new meanings) and displacement (the ability to refer to things not present in time or place). Ravens communicate about the present, not the absent or hypothetical.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens understand human speech?', a: 'Ravens can mimic human speech — reproducing words and phrases with high fidelity. Whether this involves semantic understanding (knowing what the words mean) or is purely acoustic mimicry is uncertain. Some captive ravens respond consistently to specific words (trained associations), but this is Pavlovian conditioning, not linguistic comprehension. Ravens trained to associate a word with a specific object may demonstrate something approaching semantic understanding of that word, but the kind of open-ended comprehension seen in some chimpanzee or dolphin studies has not been demonstrated in ravens.' },
];
